            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Consolation of Philosophy'</strong><br>\nWhile imprisoned by King Theodoric on accusations of conspiracy, Boethius wrote his most famous work, \"The Consolation of Philosophy.\" This book reflects on how fortune can change and the pursuit of virtue. It is structured as a dialogue between Boethius and Lady Philosophy, discussing issues such as predestination, free will, and the problem of evil.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Medieval Thought</strong><br>\nBoethius's writings, particularly \"The Consolation of Philosophy,\" exerted immense influence on medieval Christian philosophy. His ideas were taught in universities throughout the Middle Ages, and he was considered one of the last authorities on ancient philosophy during that time, earning him the title \"the last of the Romans and the first of the Scholastics.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Music and Mathematics</strong><br>\nBesides philosophy, Boethius made significant contributions to music and mathematics. He wrote extensively on music theory, with his work \"De institutione musica\" being one of the most important texts on the subject in the Middle Ages. He also categorized music as one of the quadrivium sciences—alongside arithmetic, geometry, and astronomy—which were the basis of medieval education.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
