          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Downfall</strong><br>\nBoethius fell from grace when he was implicated in a plot to overthrow Theodoric. The king was wary of Roman senators' loyalty, and Boethius's defense of a senator accused of treason led to his own arrest. Despite his previous service to the state and his philosophical contributions, Boethius was unable to escape the king's suspicions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Imprisonment and Death</strong><br>\nDuring his imprisonment, Boethius's philosophical works were his solace and his means of intellectual survival. He was executed in 524 AD after a year in custody. The circumstances of his death and his last days were filled with writing and contemplation, which left a lasting legacy through his final work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Boethius's Legacy</strong><br>\nThe legacy of Boethius is twofold: his attempt to preserve classical wisdom and his profound influence on the philosophical and theological landscape of medieval Europe. \"The Consolation of Philosophy\" has been studied and admired over centuries for its eloquent synthesis of a personal crisis and philosophical inquiry. His ideas continue to resonate, showing that in times of hardship, wisdom and introspection can be a guiding light.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
